
  The sharpest fork determines everything downstream:

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether other people can change your tasks. That decides whether this needs a server at all.

  - [1mMulti-user (collaborative)[0m — tasks are shared or assigned; someone else's edit is what you get notified about. Buys
    real notification semantics. Costs a backend, accounts, auth, persistence, and a sync story. Rules out staying a
    static page. Expensive to add later — retrofitting auth and a server onto a client-only app is close to a rewrite.
  - [1mSingle-user, machine-generated changes[0m — one person, but changes arrive from due dates passing, recurring tasks, or
    an integration. Notifications are real but "who cares about what" is trivial (it's all yours). Can stay fully
    client-side; a server becomes optional, added later only if you want cross-device or push. Cheap to change.
  - [1mSingle-user, self-made changes only[0m — you're the only source of change. Honestly, notifications are near-pointless
    here; you'd be telling someone what they just did. If this is the case I'd push back on the feature rather than
    build it.

  [1mMy recommendation: multi-user[0m, if the phrase "tasks they care about" means what it usually means. The word [3mthey[0m
  implies other people, and "care about" implies a subscription relationship that only earns its keep when changes come
  from someone else. But it's the expensive answer, so I want you to pick it deliberately rather than have me assume it.
